From fc97a159391b58259d3fb6777e8120aa57d88c0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:15:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] docs: correct the timescale argument and record the
 negative main-table result

An external review caught section 7.3 overstating. A receding-horizon
controller holds a state indefinitely by replanning, so the horizon does not
bound how long a behaviour can last -- it bounds how far the objective can
trade off. The 75x ratio is not a proof and is withdrawn.

What replaces it is narrower and in two parts: the QP's objective over the next
2 s scores nothing for waiting now to gain later, and its reference is
exogenous and constant, so no preference for waiting can arise inside the
optimiser either. That establishes the need for a state-dependent reference
rule -- and not that the rule must be learned, since a hand-written script is
also one. Separating those two requires the scripted arrival_condition sweep,
not an argument.

Also records what the convergence document omits: on the same 12-scenario
block the best trained seed loses to the fixed setpoint on completion (7/12
against 8/12) and on paired fuel (244.7 against 174.5 N s). Both that and the
interface evidence were measured on the defective lower layer, so neither
settles anything, but the negative comparison must travel with the positive
one.

