_predicted_terminal_active was an axial half-space with no bound on range, so
the approach corridor was imposed on a chaser 16 m from the target and 15 m off
the approach axis. The linearised facet there wants about 4.0 normalised units
of slack against a constraint_slack_limit of 2.0, so the QP reported infeasible
and the zero-wrench fallback handed the chaser to omega * r -- 1594 control
steps at h20 and 2161 at h35 over twelve seeds. The truth side gates the same
rows on the entry-disc latch alone, so the optimiser was refusing to solve
because of a region the task does not judge.

The gate now also requires ||p|| <= ||port|| + entry_axial = 6.0 m. Both terms
are frozen task parameters, so no tunable constant is introduced, and the
linearisation follows automatically because it branches on the same predicate.
Measured: infeasible steps go to zero on all 24 episodes at both horizons, and
the force impulse on the commonly completed episodes is identical to 0.1 N s.
Completion does not improve -- 8/12 either way at h20 -- which is why the
"the upper layer preserves feasibility" reading is withdrawn.

tests/test_terminal_gate.py pins the MPC gate to the truth gate outside the
entry sphere, keeps it firing inside, and keeps the axial clause intact.

evaluate_arrival_condition_script.py drives the frozen 2D action directly so
the commit-time sweep needs no checkpoint: commit_now is the bitwise Pure MPC
floor, commit_at holds the inertially frozen point until a given time.

diagnose_terminal_gate.py keeps the pre-fix predicate runnable behind
--terminal-gate half_space so the comparison stays reproducible.

