From a9ab68807bfbb20ba7b9fb1f0d1ab448ccb0573e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:25:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] docs: T10 execution order -- freeze the lower layer, then
 measure headroom

One question for the round: does a semantically correct fixed MPC still leave
task-level decision headroom. No training, no checkpoints.

S1 applies the patch and reproduces the 12-seed gate check (gate A: any
non-zero qp_infeasible_steps stops the round rather than being patched around
locally). S2 freezes the MPC. S3 runs the Pure MPC floor over 48 seeds -- the
sampler already draws radius, direction, relative speed, pointing error and
target phase, so widening the distribution is just more seeds and unfreezes
nothing. S4 sweeps the commit time on the seeds that failed and reports the
rescue rate, the shape of the feasible commit set, and the reverse cost.

Gate B is the fork: no rescues, or one constant commit time capturing all of
it, means no learned layer is needed and the round stops before any training.
Only past it do S5 (interface gain calibration, with the bitwise floor
re-verified) and S6 (three seeds from zero) happen.

Carries the negative result forward explicitly: on the T8 block the best
trained seed loses to the fixed setpoint on completion and on paired fuel.

